 ## Key Design Decisions
 
 - **Commands over HTTPS, one authoritative board per event.** Editors submit commands to the server, which assigns each committed edit one event revision. Peer-to-peer or browser-side merging was rejected: the tenets require one authoritative revision regardless of delivery route, and offline editing is a non-goal.
+
+- **Self-hosted WebSockets for change delivery.** Chosen over five-second HTTPS polling and hosted realtime service.
+
+  **Options considered:**
+  1. **Five-second HTTPS polling** — simplest stateless operation; browsers run a polling loop, store needs cursor queries, delivery stays request/response. Cost: ~5s median latency, more repeated reads, no connection state to manage.
+  2. **Self-hosted WebSockets** — persistent connections, subsecond push, no vendor bill. Cost: we own connection capacity (800 concurrent per event), reconnect logic on both sides, session lifecycle, backpressure, heartbeat monitoring.
+  3. **Hosted realtime service (Pusher, Ably, etc.)** — fastest to low-latency updates, least connection ops. Cost: recurring per-connection/message fees, provider coupling, credential management, publication reconciliation.
+
+  **Why self-hosted WebSockets:**
+  - The 15-second freshness target is the binding requirement; all three meet it. Subsecond is useful but not required.
+  - No recurring vendor fees — pilot budget favors fixed infrastructure cost over per-connection metering.
+  - No provider coupling — we control the protocol, scaling knobs, and data path. Event updates contain only stall IDs and operational statuses (no PII), so compliance doesn't push us to a vendor.
+  - Team has WebSocket operations capacity; the reconnect/catch-up logic is well-understood.
+
+  **What we're accepting (the tradeoff):**
+  - Connection management: 800 simultaneous browsers per event, opening-time bursts. Delivery node must handle handshake semaphores, send buffers, heartbeats, graceful drain.
+  - Reconnect complexity: browsers implement exponential backoff + cursor-based catch-up; delivery serves CATCH_UP replays or full snapshots on large gaps. Both sides must handle network partitions, sleep/wake, tab visibility.
+  - Operational burden: monitoring connection counts, lag metrics, outbox backlog; capacity planning for peak events.
+
+  **What we gain:**
+  - Fast updates without a realtime vendor bill.
+  - Full control over protocol evolution (frame types, compression, auth refresh).
+  - No provider SLA dependency during market hours.
+  - Simpler mental model than polling — push matches the "live board" UX.
+
+  **Downstream implications (cascade to LLDs):**
+  - Change store: must provide replayable revisions (full snapshots per revision) and a transactional publication outbox.
+  - Delivery: owns WebSocket session lifecycle, publication pipeline, heartbeat, backpressure, capacity limits.
+  - Browser sync: implements reconnect with exponential backoff, cursor persistence in sessionStorage, visible sync state (tenet: connectivity failures must be visible).
