+
+# Change Store Design
+
+## Context and Design Philosophy
+
+The change store is the authoritative source of truth for event board revisions. It accepts committed edits from the access layer, assigns each a monotonically increasing event revision number, and provides the replayable revision history and publication outbox that the self-hosted WebSocket delivery layer depends on. The design favors append-only storage with efficient cursor-based reads for both full-board snapshots and incremental catch-up.
+
+## Revision Model
+
+Each event has an independent revision sequence starting at 1. A revision captures:
+
+- **Revision number**: Monotonically increasing integer per event
+- **Timestamp**: Server-side commit timestamp (UTC)
+- **Actor ID**: The authorized editor from the access layer
+- **Command**: The original edit command (stall ID, new status, optional note)
+- **Resulting board state**: Full snapshot of all stall statuses after the edit applies
+
+Revisions are immutable once committed. The current board state is always derivable by applying revisions in order from the initial empty board.
+
+## Storage Schema
+
+### Revisions Table
+
+| Column | Type | Constraints |
+|--------|------|-------------|
+| event_id | UUID | PK, FK to events |
+| revision | BIGINT | PK, auto-increment per event_id |
+| committed_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | NOT NULL, default now() |
+| actor_id | UUID | NOT NULL, FK to coordinators |
+| command_json | JSONB | NOT NULL |
+| board_snapshot_json | JSONB | NOT NULL |
+
+Primary key: (event_id, revision). Index on (event_id, committed_at) for time-range queries.
+
+### Publication Outbox Table
+
+| Column | Type | Constraints |
+|--------|------|-------------|
+| id | BIGSERIAL | PK |
+| event_id | UUID | NOT NULL |
+| revision | BIGINT | NOT NULL |
+| payload_json | JSONB | NOT NULL |
+| created_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | NOT NULL, default now() |
+| published_at | TIMESTAMPTZ | NULLABLE |
+
+Unique index on (event_id, revision). The outbox is written transactionally with the revision commit. Delivery reads from the outbox in (event_id, revision) order and marks rows published_at when acknowledged by all active sessions for that event.
+
+## Read Indexes
+
+Two read paths are optimized:
+
+1. **Full snapshot**: Latest revision's board_snapshot_json — O(1) via `ORDER BY revision DESC LIMIT 1`
+2. **Incremental catch-up**: Revisions since a client's known cursor — range scan on (event_id, revision) where revision > cursor
+
+No secondary materialized views are needed; the revision table serves both.
+
+## Retention
+
+- **Hot**: Last 7 days of revisions (per goal) — kept in primary storage
+- **Cold**: Beyond 7 days — eligible for archive to object storage; not required for pilot
+
+Archive is a background job; it does not block the revision commit path.
+
+## Concurrency
+
+- Single-writer per event: the command handler serializes edits for a given event_id
+- Readers never block writers; MVCC provides consistent snapshots
+- Revision assignment uses `SELECT MAX(revision) + 1 FOR UPDATE` on the event's revision row, or a per-event sequence
+
+## Failure Modes
+
+- **Commit failure**: Command handler returns error to editor; no revision created
+- **Outbox write failure**: Transaction rolls back; command handler retries
+- **Delivery lag**: Outbox accumulates unread rows; delivery catches up when capacity allows
+- **Storage full**: Monitoring alerts at 70% capacity; archive job accelerates
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Revision storage | Append-only table with full board snapshot per revision | Event sourcing with projection rebuild; delta-only storage | Full snapshots make catch-up O(1) per revision; 7 days × 20 edits/sec × ~2KB = ~24GB worst case, well within pilot scope |
+| Outbox pattern | Transactional outbox in same DB | Separate message broker (Kafka, NATS) | Avoids extra infrastructure; DB transaction atomicity guarantees no lost publications |
+| Cursor granularity | Per-event revision number | Global logical timestamp; per-stall version vectors | Revision number is total order per event, matches delivery's replay contract, simple for clients |
+| Retention enforcement | Background archive job | TTL-based auto-delete; partitioning | Archive preserves investigatability; TTL risks premature loss during incidents |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Self-hosted WebSockets chosen — change store provides replayable revisions and outbox
+
+### Deferred
+1. Exact archive format and cold-storage destination (object storage vs. partitioned table)
+2. Whether to add a "revision compacted" flag for snapshots that can be reconstructed from deltas
+3. Backfill strategy for events created before pilot start
+
+## References
+
+- High-Level Design: `docs/high-level-design.md`
+- Delivery Design: `docs/intent/delivery/delivery-design.md` (downstream consumer of outbox)
+- Access Design: `docs/intent/access/access-design.md` (upstream command authorization)
