+
+# Delivery Design
+
+## Context and Design Philosophy
+
+The delivery component owns the self-hosted WebSocket layer: it manages persistent connections from browsers, publishes committed revisions from the change store's outbox to subscribed sessions, and handles reconnect with revision catch-up. It is the bridge between the authoritative revision log and the browser's live view. The design accepts connection management complexity to avoid vendor fees and coupling, targeting 800 simultaneous browsers with opening-time bursts.
+
+## WebSocket Protocol
+
+### Connection Establishment
+
+1. Browser opens `wss://host/events/{eventId}/sync?cursor={revision}`  
+   - `cursor` optional; omitted = subscribe from latest
+2. Server validates:
+   - Event exists and is published
+   - Browser has viewer or editor role for this event (via access layer)
+3. Server accepts connection, assigns session ID, sends `WELCOME` frame with:
+   - `sessionId`: server-generated opaque token
+   - `currentRevision`: latest revision number
+   - `boardSnapshot`: full board state at currentRevision (if cursor omitted or stale)
+   - `cursor`: the revision the browser will receive next (currentRevision + 1)
+
+### Frame Types (Server → Browser)
+
+| Type | Payload | Description |
+|------|---------|-------------|
+| `WELCOME` | `{ sessionId, currentRevision, boardSnapshot?, cursor }` | Sent once on connect |
+| `REVISION` | `{ revision, timestamp, actorId, command, boardSnapshot }` | New committed revision |
+| `CATCH_UP` | `{ revisions: Revision[], cursor }` | Bulk replay for reconnect |
+| `HEARTBEAT` | `{ serverTime }` | Every 15s; browser must respond within 5s |
+| `ERROR` | `{ code, message, retryable }` | Fatal or transient error |
+| `GOODBYE` | `{ reason }` | Server-initiated close |
+
+### Frame Types (Browser → Server)
+
+| Type | Payload | Description |
+|------|---------|-------------|
+| `PING` | `{ clientTime }` | Response to HEARTBEAT |
+| `RESYNC` | `{ cursor }` | Request catch-up from cursor |
+| `ACK` | `{ revision }` | Acknowledges receipt of REVISION (optional, for flow control) |
+
+### Revision Payload
+
+```json
+{
+  "revision": 42,
+  "timestamp": "2026-10-18T14:23:12.456Z",
+  "actorId": "coord-uuid",
+  "command": { "stallId": "A-12", "status": "CLOSED", "note": "Power issue" },
+  "boardSnapshot": { "A-12": "CLOSED", "A-13": "OPEN", ... }
+}
+```
+
+## Session Management
+
+### Session State
+
+Each active WebSocket connection holds:
+
+- `sessionId`: Unique per connection
+- `eventId`: The event this session subscribes to
+- `cursor`: Next revision the browser expects (initially from WELCOME)
+- `ackedRevision`: Highest revision the browser has ACKed (for flow control)
+- `connectedAt`: Timestamp
+- `lastPingAt`: Timestamp of last PING received
+
+### Capacity Limits
+
+- **Max sessions per event**: 800 (matches goal)
+- **Max total sessions**: 10,000 (40 markets × 250 avg, with headroom)
+- **Per-session send buffer**: 64 KB; backpressure closes session if exceeded
+- **Heartbeat interval**: 15 seconds; timeout 5 seconds
+
+### Session Lifecycle
+
+1. **Connect** → validate → assign sessionId → send WELCOME → add to event's session set
+2. **Active** → publish REVISION frames as outbox drains → handle PING/RESYNC
+3. **Reconnect** → browser sends RESYNC with cursor → server sends CATCH_UP → resume ACTIVE
+4. **Disconnect** (clean or timeout) → remove from event's session set → release resources
+
+## Publication Pipeline
+
+### Outbox Polling
+
+A dedicated publisher goroutine (or equivalent) per event:
+
+1. Polls change store outbox for `WHERE event_id = ? AND published_at IS NULL ORDER BY revision`
+2. For each row, constructs REVISION frame from `payload_json`
+3. Fans out to all sessions for that event with `cursor <= revision`
+4. On successful send to all sessions, marks row `published_at = now()`
+5. If any session send fails (backpressure, disconnect), retries on next poll iteration
+
+### Polling Cadence
+
+- **Base interval**: 100 ms
+- **Backoff**: Exponential up to 2 s when outbox empty
+- **Burst mode**: When >10 pending rows, poll continuously until caught up
+
+### Ordering Guarantee
+
+- Revisions published in strict (event_id, revision) order per event
+- No cross-event ordering required
+- Within an event, all sessions see revisions in the same order
+
+## Reconnect & Catch-Up
+
+### Browser-Initiated Resync
+
+Browser sends `RESYNC { cursor }` on:
+- Initial connect with stale cursor
+- Reconnect after disconnect (network change, sleep/wake, tab backgrounded)
+- Missed heartbeat recovery
+
+Server responds with `CATCH_UP { revisions: [...], cursor }` where revisions are all committed revisions from `cursor` to `currentRevision`. If gap > 500 revisions, server sends full snapshot in WELCOME instead and resets cursor.
+
+### Server-Initiated Resync
+
+If a session's `ackedRevision` lags `currentRevision` by >100, server may send `CATCH_UP` proactively to prevent unbounded buffer growth.
+
+### Cursor Semantics
+
+- Cursor = next revision the browser *expects to receive*
+- On WELCOME, cursor = currentRevision + 1
+- On each REVISION received, browser increments cursor
+- On CATCH_UP, cursor = last revision in batch + 1
+
+## Failure Handling
+
+### Network Partitions
+
+- Browser misses HEARTBEAT → server closes session after 5s timeout
+- Browser detects missed HEARTBEAT → initiates reconnect with RESYNC
+- Connectivity failures are visible (GOODBYE/ERROR frames), not silent
+
+### Outbox Backlog
+
+- If delivery falls behind, outbox accumulates
+- Publisher prioritizes oldest unread rows
+- Sessions with lag >100 receive proactive CATCH_UP
+- Monitoring alerts at >10,000 pending outbox rows per event
+
+### Duplicate Delivery
+
+- Idempotent by revision number: browser ignores REVISION where revision < cursor
+- ACK is advisory; server does not wait for ACK to mark published_at
+
+### Session Storms
+
+- Opening time: many browsers connect simultaneously
+- Connection acceptor uses semaphore (max 200 concurrent handshakes)
+- Excess connections queued with 5s timeout; reject with `ERROR { code: "CAPACITY", retryable: true }`
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Protocol | Custom binary-like JSON over WebSocket | GraphQL subscriptions; Server-Sent Events; MQTT over WebSocket | Custom keeps frame overhead low (~200B/revision), no extra protocol layers, full control over reconnect semantics |
+| Outbox polling | Per-event publisher goroutine polling DB | LISTEN/NOTIFY (Postgres); CDC (Debezium); separate message broker | Polling is simple, no extra deps, works with any SQL DB; 100ms base interval meets 15s freshness easily |
+| Catch-up granularity | Full revision replay (not delta) | Delta-only; snapshot + delta | Revisions already carry full board snapshot; replay is simple and correct |
+| Flow control | Optional ACK, per-session send buffer | Credit-based; TCP backpressure only | ACK enables application-level visibility; buffer prevents OOM on slow clients |
+| Heartbeat | Server-initiated every 15s, 5s timeout | Browser-initiated; WebSocket ping/pong | Server controls timing, detects dead clients proactively, works through proxies that drop idle connections |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Self-hosted WebSockets — delivery owns session lifecycle and publication
+
+### Deferred
+1. Exact binary frame format (MessagePack vs JSON) — JSON for pilot, evaluate MessagePack if bandwidth becomes concern
+2. Horizontal scaling of delivery nodes (sticky sessions vs. shared session store)
+3. Per-session compression (permessage-deflate) — evaluate after pilot metrics
+4. Authentication token refresh during long-lived sessions (8h session expiry from access layer)
+
